Anomalous diffusion in turbulent flows is hypothesized to be governed by a fractional diffusion equation whose L\'{e}vy exponent $\alpha$ is determined by the Eulerian spectral roughness $\xi$ of the velocity field. We investigate the validity and observability of this theoretical link using a comprehensive numerical analysis across several turbulence models. We utilize data from synthetic multifractal energy cascades, the Kraichnan model, and the deterministic Lorenz-96 system. Our methodology involves computing Eulerian structure functions to determine the spectral roughness and applying robust statistical estimators to Lagrangian trajectory data to measure the effective L\'{e}vy exponent. A sliding-window analysis is employed to track the Renormalization Group (RG) flow of the exponent over time, probing its convergence to the predicted asymptotic fixed point. Our results highlight that the emergence of the predicted fractional dynamics is an asymptotic phenomenon subject to strong system constraints. For the Kraichnan model, we find that finite simulation size and time prevent the system from reaching the anomalous fixed point, with the RG flow showing the exponent remains near the Gaussian value of two. Furthermore, analysis of one-dimensional models reveals that topological trapping can induce strong subdiffusion, a behavior not captured by the standard theory. We conclude that the proposed relationship between Eulerian roughness and the Lagrangian fractional operator describes a valid asymptotic state, but its manifestation in physical or numerical systems is critically dependent on sufficient scale separation, simulation time, and spatial dimensionality to overcome pre-asymptotic effects and geometric constraints.

